title: Storage
kind: overview
aliases: store lmdb kv kvstore
summary: Each repository's metadata and history live in a key-value store (LMDB) on disk; nothing proportional to the repository stays in memory.

A repository's metarecords, their fields and the event log live in its own
''key-value store'' — LMDB, through the `heed` crate — under
`.metafolder/internal/kv/`. The store is memory-mapped: nothing is read at open,
the operating system's page cache keeps the pages in use, and nothing whose size
grows with the repository is held in the daemon's memory. A repository of any size
works on an ordinary machine; one that fits in memory ends up entirely in the page
cache on its own.

The store holds two kinds of data: the ''primary'' data (metarecords, rows, the
log, HEAD), the source of truth; and ''derived'' key spaces (ids, bitmaps,
partitions, the forest's bitmaps, trigrams), which answer queries and can always
be rebuilt from the primary data. A bug in derived data gives wrong answers, never
lost data — and a check detects it.

Queries are answered from the store directly: there is no resident index to build
at load and no SQL engine (SQLite was dropped in September 2026).

